values
  (194, 'How 193 is built (slice 2.3, `slice-2-3.md`). Confirms 22''s framework: `apps/web` is Next.js 16.4.5 (App Router), pinned, its own Vercel project with root `apps/web`; the API''s build does not include it. The web app''s server proves the person to the API with a person token: an HS256 JWT signed with `WEB_API_SECRET`, shared only by the two servers, with `sub` (Slack user id), `team`, `name`, `aud` `hippidy-api`, `iss` `hippidy-web`, living two minutes; the API refuses one older than five minutes or for another workspace than `SLACK_WORKSPACE_ID`. `jose` 6.2.15 in both apps. The session is a stateless cookie, an HS256 JWT signed with a second secret, `WEB_SESSION_SECRET`, for eight hours, HttpOnly, Secure and SameSite=Lax; nothing revokes it sooner. `state` and `nonce` travel in a signed cookie that lives ten minutes and is sent only to the callback. The workspace is checked twice: the `https://slack.com/team_id` claim of Slack''s id token at sign-in, and `team` in every person token. A person may call `GET /v1/me` and the read routes `GET /v1/conversations*`; every other route stays on the service token. Sign-out is a POST', 'A token per call, signed by the web app''s server, keeps the browser away from anything the API accepts and needs no new table. Two secrets, so a leaked session cookie secret cannot mint API calls. A stateless session needs no storage in the web app, which must reach data only through the API (22); eight hours bounds what a person who left the workspace keeps, which is acceptable for a pilot of trusted people (175). `jose` is a small, dependency-free library that verifies Slack''s RS256 keys and both HS256 tokens', 'active', 'the owner', '2026-11-25T00:00:00Z', 'import', '194')
on conflict (origin, origin_ref) where origin_ref is not null do nothing;
